  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "IRR with a direct non-number gives #NUM!, and the guess is still checked",
  "statement": "Text or a logical typed directly as the values argument of IRR is not an error of type: \"\", \"2\", \"x\", \"0\", \"-2\", TRUE and FALSE all give #NUM!, never #VALUE!. The guess is read anyway, and its error wins: with any of those values, a guess of \"x\" gives #VALUE! and a guess of NA() gives #N/A, while 0.1 or an empty guess leaves #NUM!. A direct error in values wins over the guess: IRR(NA(),\"x\") is #N/A.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "40 recorded readings: seven direct non-numbers and NA() as values, each with five guesses (none, 0.1, \"x\", NA(), empty).",
  "inferred": null,
  "documentation": null,
